# WHITELIST, not a blocklist.
#
# This directory is a working root that also contains 20-odd checked-out
# repositories and a large amount of one-off material generated while solving
# problems -- notes, probe scripts, patched copies of other people's
# launch files, logs. Almost none of it is something this repository should
# carry, and a blocklist would let the next one through by default.
#
# So: everything is ignored, and the bootstrap is added back by name. If you
# add a file that belongs here, add it below too.

*

# --- the bootstrap itself --------------------------------------------------
!.gitignore
# Line endings, pinned to LF. Trees copied by hand from a Windows workstation
# can arrive with every .py file rewritten to CRLF (one copy had 224) -- every
# one of which then reads as an uncommitted change. It has to be TRACKED to
# travel with the bootstrap, which this whitelist would otherwise prevent.
!.gitattributes
!README.md
!tari.bash
!dgpy_install.sh
!colcon_defaults.yaml
!container.repos
!sim.repos
!acq.repos
!hw.repos
!site.repos
!workspace.repos

# --- who approves a release ------------------------------------------------
# Names the reviewer GitHub requires on a pull request into a cleared_* branch,
# which is what publishes to the public mirror.
!.github/
!.github/CODEOWNERS

# --- the public form of the same manifests ---------------------------------
# What somebody outside the organisation builds from: the public mirrors over
# HTTPS, no account and no SSH key. Generated from the manifests above by
# tools/make_public_manifests.py and committed, so a public user can run plain
# `vcs import < public/sim.repos` without this repository's tooling.
!public/
!public/*.repos
!tools/
!tools/make_public_manifests.py

# --- checks over the bootstrap ---------------------------------------------
# Static checks on the scripts above -- no target environment, no ROS, nothing
# installed:  python3 -m pytest test/
!test/
!test/test_install_policy.py
!test/test_script_robustness.py
!test/test_extension_portability.py
!test/test_sourcing_uniformity.py
# These run real git repositories, real Python and real file modes in a tmpdir
# rather than only reading the scripts, but still need no network, no ROS and
# nothing installed.
!test/test_sources_resume.py
!test/test_env_repair.py
!test/test_line_endings.py
!test/test_shared_workspace.py
# Runs dgpy_install.sh itself, with package_status() stubbed, to pin which
# --check states are fatal. No network, no ROS, nothing installed.
!test/test_check_exit_status.py
# Drives tari.bash's own --dry-run to pin where the NORMS metadata plug-in is
# installed and on which layer. Installs nothing and needs no privilege.
!test/test_norms_plugin_install.py
# Runs the public-manifest generator's --check, so a manifest edited without
# regenerating fails the suite, and asserts that the public form has no SSH
# URL, no private repository and nothing dropped without a stated reason.
!test/test_public_manifests.py


# --- the container ---------------------------------------------------------
# The image definitions and the lifecycle script are one unit and now live in
# one repository, imported as the `container` layer into <workspace>/container.
# Only the forwarding stub is tracked here, so that ./ros2_docker.sh works
# straight after cloning the bootstrap and says how to get the rest.
!ros2_docker.sh
# The image's last layer installs the dataguzzler packages, so the build context
# has to be the whole working root -- which can reach 27 GB of checkouts, scan
# data and build trees. This allowlist cuts it to about 39 MB. It is
# LOAD-BEARING and belongs with the bootstrap: without it the build sends the
# whole working root, and a fresh clone would have no idea why.
!.dockerignore

# --- never ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated by tari.bash --phase env, machine-specific by construction.
tari_setup.bash
